File the production forest's shape, and repair a punch-list row I truncated (P-280, P-285)

The anchor. Every P-280 figure came off a synthetic bundle this codebase
generated, so it could only contain assumptions the code already held. This is
the first measurement of the owner's own forest that can contradict them, taken
by him inside the production environment on 2026-09-25 with build 3b3e6c0.
Stored at next/artifacts/p280-shape-profile/ with its full reading, and
force-added past the artifacts ignore the way p279-before's small results were.

The file is kept exactly as it came out. Editing a measurement by hand is how a
figure stops being checkable, which is the same reason a golden hash that needs
changing is a finding rather than a fix. It carries the capture's name on line 2
- the defect 419f133 fixed - so the README says plainly that this copy is not for
redistribution, and records the fingerprint e245e7645e764785 that the current
build reports for the same capture so a future profile can be tied to this one.
The capture is already named in the specification, so nothing is newly exposed
by keeping it here.

WHAT IT SETTLED.

The occurrence count is confirmed by two independent routes: 330,856,576,
identical to what --judgement-forecast reported on 2026-09-24 from a separately
written pass. This is a real confirmation, unlike the withdrawn "two routes
agreeing within 1.3%" claim, which was cancellation.

P-285 is measured: 20,454,613 occurrences carry S-1-5-10, 6.2% of the total.
P-279 took permission_entry from 17,164,866 rows to 24,098 on the bench; on this
forest SELF alone would put up to twenty million back, because the resolved
trustee name is inside the sharing identity. That moves P-285 from a tidy-up to
the question of whether P-279 worked at all outside the laboratory, and it is the
figure the new sharing unit has to be chosen against.

Reuse is far more skewed than its mean. The median descriptor is carried by ONE
object and the largest by 4,568, against a mean of 2.46. Shape C still cuts 330
million rows to 141 million, but the saving comes from a small number of
enormously shared descriptors rather than from a broad average, and anything
designed around "descriptors are shared about twice" will be wrong about half the
table.

Nothing failed to parse: all 659,196 stored descriptors read completely, so
P-284's distinction was worth making and the honest answer here is none.
Separately, 280,871 objects - just under a third of the forest - have no
descriptor stored at all, which is not the same as holding no permissions.

AND ONE THING IT CORRECTS. The specification recorded "Every name is ASCII -
106.1 bytes and 106.1 characters", concluded from a byte mean and a character
mean agreeing to one decimal place. They agree because a handful of accented
names cannot move a mean across 659,196 objects. The profile compares the two
lengths per object and answers EveryNameIsAscii: false. Nothing rests on it -
the code already pays in bytes - but a figure that looked checked was not, which
is the shape this page keeps recording about itself.

A LIMITATION OF THE PROFILE, RECORDED SO IT IS NOT READ AS A FINDING. 904
distinct object-type GUIDs are outside the catalogue shipped with the product,
covering 150,184,579 occurrences. That is not 904 customer extensions: the
shipped catalogue is a curated set of security-relevant GUIDs rather than the
whole Active Directory schema, so most will be ordinary attributes it does not
list. The profile cannot tell an unlisted standard GUID from a local extension
and names neither. Step 2b needs the distinction; the product does not, because
capture reads the forest's schema partition (decision 0014).

